WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:05.649
From doubtful executives to his own personal
demons, actor and filmmaker Jason Mewes has

00:00:05.649 --> 00:00:10.080
beaten the odds to become a cult favorite
Hollywood icon, known to many, beloved by

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:10.600
all.

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:14.019
Here's how it all happened — and what might
happen next.

00:00:15.129 --> 00:00:19.370
Jason Mewes was born in the small town of
Highlands, New Jersey, and grew up having

00:00:19.370 --> 00:00:24.230
essentially no ambitions of ever becoming
an actor, director, or a stoner icon in the

00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:25.540
vein of Cheech and Chong.

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:30.440
In Highlands, Mewes and his longtime collaborator
Kevin Smith lived only a couple of blocks

00:00:30.450 --> 00:00:34.250
away from each other and attended the same
high school, but they weren't necessarily

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:35.500
friends right away.

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:39.820
For one, the younger Mewes was still a student
after Smith had already graduated.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:44.900
For another, Smith and his circle of friends
frequently found him to be, in Mewes' words,

00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:46.240
"obnoxious and weird."

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:51.120
But over time, Smith bonded with the oddball
freshman out of a shared love of comics and

00:00:51.130 --> 00:00:56.160
pop culture, and an increasing appreciation
on Smith's part for Jay's oddball — and

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:58.580
sometimes profane — sense of humor.

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:03.380
Despite Mewes showing no real interest in
theater or performance as a teenager, Smith

00:01:03.380 --> 00:01:06.400
would often remark to Mewes that someone should
put him in a movie.

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:10.860
It was only fitting that Smith, an aspiring
filmmaker himself, would be the one to do

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:11.360
it.

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:16.159
Jason Mewes got his first brush with fame
when Smith wrote a role for him in what would

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:18.600
become his debut feature, Clerks.

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:22.600
In his first appearance as the character,
Jay is barely a performance.

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:23.580
He's just…

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:24.240
Jay.

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:29.360
Being interviewed for a documentary commemorating
the tenth anniversary of Clerks, Smith recalled,

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:33.360
"I gave him the script and he read it and
I was like, 'What do you think?'

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:34.580
He's like, 'I don't know if I can do this.'

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:35.440
I'm like, 'Dude, it's you!'"

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:40.279
Though close to the character and naturally
charismatic, Mewes still had to confront stage

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:42.260
fright when it came time to shoot the movie.

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:46.680
In the beginning, he was so nervous in front
of the camera that he at times asked the crew

00:01:46.689 --> 00:01:51.310
to leave during his performances, meaning
some scenes of Clerks were shot with literally

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:52.800
nobody behind the camera.

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:57.000
Mewes said in the Clerks making-of doc that
his youthful antics had always been about

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:59.140
entertaining himself, not others.

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:02.159
As soon as an audience was involved, he froze
up.

00:02:02.260 --> 00:02:08.780
"Like as soon as I got an audience, I just
felt really silly doing it for other people,

00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:11.340
and just being like, 'All right everyone,
get ready!

00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:14.440
That anxiousness had unexpected benefits,
though.

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:18.900
Mewes' inability to sell a crucial line in
a climactic scene is what inspired Silent

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:24.680
Bob's one line of dialogue in the film, establishing
Bob in series canon as a mostly mute character

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:26.840
who only speaks when he has wisdom to drop.

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:29.560
"You know there's a million fine lookin women
in the world.

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:31.580
They don't all bring you lasagna at work.

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:33.140
Most of em just cheat on ya."

00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:35.400
… "C'mon, limp d---."

00:02:36.829 --> 00:02:41.100
The sale of Clerks to Miramax meant that Jason
Mewes' performance as Jay would be seen by

00:02:41.110 --> 00:02:44.129
many more people than his circle of friends
in New Jersey.

00:02:44.129 --> 00:02:49.819
Suddenly, this $27,000 indie film was on its
way to becoming a bona fide cult classic — and

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:51.280
Hollywood was noticing.

00:02:51.460 --> 00:02:56.140
Smith's second film, Mallrats, was fast-tracked
into production with the backing of a major

00:02:56.140 --> 00:02:56.640
studio.

00:02:56.820 --> 00:03:01.420
Unlike Clerks, filmed with a skeleton crew
over nearly a month's worth of night shoots,

00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:05.670
Mallrats was a real deal production with real
deal money behind it — and the people putting

00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:07.680
up the money had opinions.

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:12.100
Specifically, they liked the character of
Jay, but they didn't really like the person

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:13.120
playing Jay.

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:18.500
This lead to an unenviable situation in which
Mewes had to audition for a chance to essentially

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:19.580
play himself.

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:25.460
In the suits' view, Smith was a wunderkind,
but Mewes was an amateur, and they misunderstood,

00:03:25.460 --> 00:03:27.140
or underestimated, his appeal.

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:31.579
The studio tried to recast the role with a
more established comic actor like Breckin

00:03:31.580 --> 00:03:32.800
Meyer or Seth Green.

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:37.240
While Smith won the right to cast Mewes in
Mallrats, there were strings attached: if

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:41.579
Mewes' performance wasn't satisfying on day
one, he'd be fired and replaced.

00:03:41.740 --> 00:03:45.860
It was only when the executives got a look
at Mewes in action that they realized he was

00:03:45.860 --> 00:03:47.280
simply irreplaceable.

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:50.100
"The whole f----- world's against us, dude,
I swear to god."

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:54.939
Despite the troubles he was put through by
the studio, Jason Mewes maintains that Mallrats

00:03:54.940 --> 00:03:57.480
was his favorite one of Smith's films to work
on.

00:03:57.610 --> 00:04:03.820
"Clerks was the first movie, but to me, really,
Mallrats is my first movie, because it was

00:04:03.829 --> 00:04:05.519
like it became real."

00:04:05.519 --> 00:04:09.840
While Mewes would go on to spend his adulthood
in show business, the real-life act of moviemaking

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:12.520
was still a novelty to him at the time of
Mallrats.

00:04:12.700 --> 00:04:18.170
“I'm in Minnesota, staying in a hotel, and
there's wardrobe, and there's stunt guys,

00:04:18.170 --> 00:04:23.440
and I get to hang from a grappling hook, and
I get to meet Stan Lee for the first time."

00:04:23.620 --> 00:04:28.160
Mewes would later go on to cross paths with
the Marvel Comics magnate on multiple occasions

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:32.380
in the years to come, even casting him for
a cameo for his own movie, Madness in the

00:04:32.380 --> 00:04:33.380
Method, in 2019.

00:04:33.380 --> 00:04:35.060
"Boy, I heard he's a knucklehead."

00:04:35.420 --> 00:04:39.840
Though Mallrats didn't exactly make bank at
the box office, Smith got another chance to

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:44.820
make a movie, this time mining more personal
territory for the less slapsticky, more artsy

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:45.780
Chasing Amy.

00:04:45.980 --> 00:04:50.100
This story had less room for Jay and Silent
Bob, though the characters do appear in a

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:51.120
pivotal scene.

00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:55.920
The movie also introduced comic book alter
egos for the characters: the marijuana-themed

00:04:55.920 --> 00:04:58.040
superheroes Bluntman and Chronic.

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:03.100
It's the in-universe creators of those characters
that the story of Chasing Amy revolves around.

00:05:03.340 --> 00:05:08.070
But despite his increasing notoriety, Mewes
continued working "regular guy" jobs until

00:05:08.070 --> 00:05:10.660
well after you'd think he would have hung
it up to go Hollywood.

00:05:10.780 --> 00:05:15.260
"I did Chasing Amy, and I went back to delivering
pizza and doing construction and stuff.

00:05:15.260 --> 00:05:17.270
So I worked a regular job for years.

00:05:17.270 --> 00:05:22.480
It really wasn't until after Dogma that I
stopped working construction and delivering

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:23.240
pizza."

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:28.280
Mewes reached the height of the zeitgeist
in 2001, when he starred in Jay and Silent

00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:32.860
Bob Strike Back, the first film in Smith's
universe to revolve entirely around the duo.

00:05:32.920 --> 00:05:37.200
But that professional success didn't necessarily
reflect a healthy personal life.

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:42.250
By then, Mewes had spent years going down
the path of drug addiction, specifically to

00:05:42.250 --> 00:05:42.740
opiates.

00:05:42.940 --> 00:05:47.320
Mewes was born to a mother with addiction
issues of her own, and spent his early adolescence

00:05:47.330 --> 00:05:51.550
practicing a straight-edge lifestyle — an
identity which had thoroughly eroded by the

00:05:51.550 --> 00:05:53.780
time he and Smith teamed up for Clerks.

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:58.380
But what started out as perfectly innocent,
even charming substance abuse had become a

00:05:58.380 --> 00:06:01.620
bleak, life-or-death situation by the early
2000s.

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:06.880
An arrest for heroin possession in 1999 was
compounded by another arrest warrant, issued

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:10.580
in 2001, over a failure to make a court date
in New Jersey.

00:06:10.580 --> 00:06:15.020
The circumstances led Smith to decline to
cast Mewes in his sixth film, Jersey Girl

00:06:15.020 --> 00:06:19.440
— an unfortunate first for the frequent
collaborators, whose friendly relationship

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:22.200
was being strained to the breaking point by
Mewes' addiction.

00:06:22.520 --> 00:06:26.890
The warrant ultimately led Jason Mewes to
return to New Jersey and face the music, dealing

00:06:26.890 --> 00:06:30.060
with the legal problem, and perhaps his addictions
as well.

00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:31.100
“I got fed up.

00:06:31.100 --> 00:06:34.200
I was like, I have to take care of this warrant,
because even if I get sober and I'm doing

00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:38.620
good in California, this warrant will hang
over my head forever.

00:06:38.620 --> 00:06:40.880
So I went there and took care of it."

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:45.360
Mewes was sentenced to four to six months
of rehab in New Jersey, with a period of probation

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:45.960
to follow.

00:06:46.180 --> 00:06:50.720
His surrender to authorities started a process
of recovery that continues to this day.

00:06:50.720 --> 00:06:55.080
He credits the success to his support system,
staying busy with work and surrounding himself

00:06:55.080 --> 00:06:57.660
with friends who aren't shy about keeping
him accountable.

00:06:57.760 --> 00:07:00.120
"Keeping busy is a big thing.

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:05.340
[...] I realized for me, the people who really
care, it's, 'Hey, why do you have that beer

00:07:05.340 --> 00:07:06.320
in your hand.

00:07:06.320 --> 00:07:08.220
And I'm like, 'Oh, I'm just passing it to
so-and-so.'

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:12.360
But a friend who's like, 'Hey, you've been
sober two years and you're really addicted

00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:14.140
to drugs, not booze.

00:07:14.140 --> 00:07:16.020
You can have a beer.

00:07:16.020 --> 00:07:17.020
You can have one beer.'

00:07:17.020 --> 00:07:21.530
That to me isn't… he might be a friend,
but not really.

00:07:21.530 --> 00:07:24.070
So that to me was a big deal.

00:07:24.070 --> 00:07:28.340
Surrounding myself with people who knew, no,
I can't have a beer, because then I'll be

00:07:28.340 --> 00:07:32.200
smoking crack in a month, and I'll disappear."

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:37.800
In the years since he committed to sobriety,
Jason Mewes has continued to work as an actor,

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:40.580
being cast in many smaller movies that you
may not have heard of.

00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:46.160
In 2010, Mewes was also a cast member on Todd
and the Book of Pure Evil, a Canadian comedy

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:48.560
series that was canceled after two seasons.

00:07:48.880 --> 00:07:52.670
Much like Mewes' early movies, the show is
something of a cult favorite with a small

00:07:52.670 --> 00:07:54.000
but dedicated fanbase.

00:07:54.160 --> 00:07:57.120
“I think when people see it they love it,
it's just them finding it.

00:07:57.120 --> 00:07:59.220
But yes, Todd and the Book of Pure Evil.

00:07:59.220 --> 00:08:02.360
Definitely I believe you guys will be able
to find it somewhere."

00:08:02.360 --> 00:08:07.250
Another lesser-known film Mewes is proud of
is Zombie Hamlet, which is more or less exactly

00:08:07.250 --> 00:08:08.370
what it sounds like.

00:08:08.370 --> 00:08:11.320
"I'm shocked more people didn't see that,
because it was a really funny movie."

00:08:12.300 --> 00:08:16.360
Out of the dozens of projects Jason Mewes
has been involved with over the years, one

00:08:16.360 --> 00:08:20.770
he's most proud of is his directorial debut,
2019's Madness in the Method.

00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:26.420
The movie was born out of two desires: breaking
out of the stoner niche as an actor, and stepping

00:08:26.420 --> 00:08:29.080
behind the camera for a substantial feature
project.

00:08:29.370 --> 00:08:34.440
Prior to Madness, Mewes had only directed
a few music videos and shorts, experiences

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:35.340
he enjoyed.

00:08:35.580 --> 00:08:36.990
But they left him wanting more.

00:08:37.100 --> 00:08:41.240
Soon enough, he was on independent film sets
with his mind wandering toward bigger, more

00:08:41.240 --> 00:08:43.120
involved aspects of the production.

00:08:43.469 --> 00:08:47.340
The film is a kind of meta-narrative that
takes place in a slightly heightened version

00:08:47.340 --> 00:08:51.780
of the real world, following Mewes as he attempts
to learn method acting with the help of a

00:08:51.780 --> 00:08:52.980
near-mythical guidebook.

00:08:53.090 --> 00:08:57.880
Owing partly to the character's addictive
personality, things get out of hand quickly.

00:08:57.890 --> 00:09:02.390
"I read the book [...] I'm told to read it
slow and really soak it in, and I read it

00:09:02.390 --> 00:09:03.000
too fast.

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:08.700
And then from there, an accident happens,
and then a killing spree happens.

00:09:08.700 --> 00:09:11.380
And it's a comedy drama."

00:09:12.160 --> 00:09:17.780
On top of his directorial debut being released,
2019 also proved big for Jason Mewes in another,

00:09:17.780 --> 00:09:21.480
more familiar way, with the filmmaker stepping
back into the Jay role.

00:09:21.640 --> 00:09:26.890
The part has been revived as a part of Smith's
13th movie, Jay and Silent Bob Reboot, a sequel

00:09:26.890 --> 00:09:31.230
to Strike Back that Smith has called, quote,
"literally the same f—ing movie all over

00:09:31.230 --> 00:09:31.840
again."

00:09:32.100 --> 00:09:33.600
Well, if you liked it the first time…

00:09:33.720 --> 00:09:39.320
“Alright guys, welcome to behind the scenes
of the set of Mallrats 2.”

00:09:39.380 --> 00:09:45.100
In typical ironic fashion, the movie is effectively
a reboot that lampoons reboots, following

00:09:45.110 --> 00:09:50.180
Jay and Silent Bob as they head back to Hollywood,
again, seeking sweet revenge for the industry's

00:09:50.180 --> 00:09:52.080
slights against them — again.

00:09:52.180 --> 00:09:57.040
This time, they're out to stop a blockbuster
reimagining of Bluntman and Chronic, now starring

00:09:57.040 --> 00:10:00.600
former Batman Val Kilmer and current Supergirl
Melissa Benoist.

00:10:00.680 --> 00:10:05.340
And while Jay went sober as his namesake for
Clerks II, the sequel seems to have the fictional

00:10:05.340 --> 00:10:08.740
character back in the mood of rolling fatties
and smoking blunts.

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:13.500
Outside of filmmaking, Mewes has also found
a second unexpected niche as a podcaster and

00:10:13.500 --> 00:10:18.440
a stage performer, touring with the stand-up
show "Jay Mewes and His A-Mewes-ing Stories."

00:10:18.680 --> 00:10:22.380
He's also begun streaming content on Twitch
under the name JayMewes.

00:10:22.740 --> 00:10:24.260
"Yeah man, it's been fun.

00:10:24.260 --> 00:10:30.460
It's like between this movie and reboot, and
streaming, and the stand up comedy, it's been

00:10:30.460 --> 00:10:30.980
good."

